<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Sigismund Zápolya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1540">1540</a>–<a href="/years/1571">1571</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as John II of Hungary, John Sigismund</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Sigismund Zápolya was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-zapolya">John Zápolya</a>, king of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-jagiellon">Isabella Jagiellon</a>, elected king as an infant in 1540 under Ottoman protection. He ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alba-iulia">Gyulafehérvár</a> and supported the Diet of Torda's decree of religious freedom in 1568. By the Treaty of Speyer in 1570 he gave up the royal title and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-transylvania">Prince of Transylvania</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-sigismund-zapolya.jpg" alt="John Sigismund Zápolya" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dominicus Custos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jan_Zygmunt_Zapolya.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John Sigismund was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> on 7 July 1540, the only son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-zapolya">John Zápolya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-jagiellon">Isabella Jagiellon</a>. His father had been elected king in 1526 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Battle of Mohács</a>, while a rival group of lords had proclaimed Ferdinand I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a>. The Treaty of Várad of 1538 confirmed Ferdinand's right to reunite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> after John's death, but on his deathbed John Zápolya made those present swear to prevent the transfer of his realm to Ferdinand. The Diet elected the infant king on 13 September 1540, though he was never crowned with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-crown-of-hungary">Holy Crown of Hungary</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Sultan Suleiman invaded, saying he came to protect the child, and in 1541 his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissaries</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> without opposition. Suleiman let John Sigismund keep the lands east of the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a> in exchange for a yearly tribute of 10,000 florins. The family settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alba-iulia">Gyulafehérvár</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, but in 1551 his father's treasurer, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-martinuzzi">George Martinuzzi</a>, forced Isabella to give up her son's realm to Ferdinand in return for two Silesian duchies and 140,000 florins, and they went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. At Suleiman's urging the Transylvanian Diet called them back in 1556. Isabella ruled in her son's name until her death in 1559, and then he ruled for himself under the title king-elect.</p><p>A powerful lord, Melchior Balassa, deserted to Ferdinand in 1561, and most counties outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> followed. The Székely commoners also rose, and the king's army crushed them in 1562. John Sigismund paid homage to Suleiman at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zemun">Zemun</a> in 1566, and the Treaty of Adrianople of 1568 confirmed him in Transylvania and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partium">Partium</a>. Originally Catholic, he converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> in 1562 and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a> in 1564. Influenced by his physician Giorgio Biandrata and his court preacher Ferenc Dávid, he later adopted Anti-Trinitarian views, becoming the only Unitarian monarch in history.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1568 the Diet at Torda passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-torda">Edict of Torda</a>, declaring that nobody should suffer for religious reasons and that faith is a gift of God. It expanded religious freedom beyond the standards of 16th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. By the Treaty of Speyer of 16 August 1570 John Sigismund acknowledged Maximilian II as the sole king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-transylvania">Prince of Transylvania</a> and Lord of Parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>. He died childless in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alba-iulia">Gyulafehérvár</a> on 14 March 1571 and was buried in St Michael's Cathedral in Gyulafehérvár according to Unitarian rite. The Diet elected the Catholic Stephen Báthory to succeed him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Sigismund Zápolya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Zápolya's death in 1540, his widow Isabella Jagiellon and the bishop George Martinuzzi governed for the infant <b>John Sigismund</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-hungarian-kingdom">Eastern Hungarian Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Speyer in 1570, <b>John Sigismund Zápolya</b> renounced the Hungarian royal title and became Prince of Transylvania, ruling Transylvania and the Partium, eastern Hungarian counties, under Ottoman suzerainty with his court at Gyulafehérvár.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-transylvania">Principality of Transylvania</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>John Sigismund</b> renounced the royal title by the Treaty of Speyer in 1570, and the land became the Principality of Transylvania under Ottoman overlordship.</blockquote><a href="/state/voivodeship-of-transylvania">Voivodeship of Transylvania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alba-iulia">Alba Iulia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.47,19.05&amp;year=1560" aria-label="Buda on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4995 499 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Sigismund Zápolya"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5445 769h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5445 769h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.47,19.05&amp;year=1560">What was here in 1560? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
